A||||10||JohnnyK||Rust inside my distributor||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||08-21-2000||04:08 PM||||Well, I finally was able to break the dist. cap off, and noticed that there is rust on those "round things" in there. (Don't know what they are) The things that the rotor mounts to. Almost looks like there is a green coil wrapped around it or something. Anyways, I can't afford to buy a new distributor ($215) so is this serious? I don't know how it looks, but it looks like there are "arms" coming off the round things (contacts I assume) that are rusted. Can I just wire brush this off? Also, I tried to take off the whole distributor, by undoing that 13mm bolt and taking off that bracket, but I couldn't pull, or twist it off. should it just come off, or is it rusted on there or soemthing? Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||08-21-2000||04:15 PM||||Yes, it sounds like the dist has rusted into it's hole in the block. That's a problem only if the timing is off or the dist is bad. ((This is a common problem on these 4 cyls. Especially in road salt land.))<P>The rust on the "round things" and "arms" isn't a big deal. leave it alone. Trying to remove it could actually cause more problems than it might prevent.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||08-21-2000||05:10 PM||||You know what I mean by those "arms" though? Seems like this would stop the electricty from travelling and making spark? Unless those parts have nothing to do with it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||gixxer||08-21-2000||06:14 PM||||The rust stuff IS rust, and conductive if it gets into the wrong areas. The dist. on my V6 was filthy with it. I removed the loose stuff with an old nylon bristle toothbrush. I wouldn't use a wire brush. There is no electric current running thru the stator and pole pieces worth worrying about. They are there to create a signal to the module using magnetic fields. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ed||08-22-2000||01:19 AM||||The "arms" are part of the pick up coil.  I trust theogre's judgement. So follow his advice in your case because you can't afford to fix it, and the car's running (I assume). <P>For others reading this post, if it was me, however, I'd replace or rebuild the distributor.  It sounds to me like several things are about to fail.  The "rust" on the pick up coil is a bad sign, and pickup coils do fail.  I don't know if I'd brush it with anything, because if you're careless you might break a wire. Gently blow the "rust" away.  I can't remember for sure if the fours have a vent screen on the distributor base, but if it does, gently clean it too.    ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jake_Dragon||02-06-2001||07:42 PM||||AutoZone <P>$100 New! There is a $50 core so bring your old one.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Old Lar||02-06-2001||08:38 PM||||After 13 years, my distributor's inside looked like that.  I put an Associated Auto remanufactured one in.  The car ran like new.<P>Remember the Fiero is an aging car and if you want it to run, you're going to have to replace parts that wear out.||||reg||3
Z||000007||JohnnyK||02-06-2001||11:27 PM||||What?? geez man, I solved that problem ages ago.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> It's amazing what a little sandpaper can do for your car. I'd rather not buy new parts if I don't have to.||||reg||1
Z||000008||strick8||02-07-2001||11:35 AM||||You can rebuild it with parts for <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fierostore.com</A>  The main shaft with magnet is $64.70 and the Ignition pick is $7.60 Dont forget a new O-ring also $1.40||||reg||10
Z||000009||terryk||02-07-2001||12:07 PM||||Any of the dist from a Celebrity 2.8L will work. There are billions in the junkyards.||||reg||1
Z||000010||JohnnyK||02-07-2001||02:54 PM||||Or, I could go down to my local auto store and buy the 0-ring that cost me $0.37 (canadian too.) I guarantee your stores will have it, and buy some sandpaper.. Good as new!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
